  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Below normal temperatures will continue through the \nupcoming week, with a few showers possible at times mid-week.\n\nOver the next few days the bulk of broad upper level troughing over \neastern Canada will slowly drift eastward and out to sea. As it does \nso...the far southwestern extent of this trough will eventually \nbreak off and merge with a pair of shortwaves making their way \nacross the Ohio Valley...resulting in the formation of a modest \nclosed low that will then slide southeastward across the Ohio \nValley/mid-Atlantic states during the mid-late week period. \nMeanwhile at the surface...expansive cool Canadian high pressure \nwill slowly drift from Ontario to Quebec Provinces and strengthen \nthrough Wednesday...then will remain in place through the end of the \nweek while a surface low develops off the mid-Atlantic coastline in \nresponse to the approaching cutoff upper low and attendant \nDCVA/height falls aloft.\n\nAs a result of all this...our region will remain under a prevailing \nnortheasterly to easterly low level flow right through the week... \nresulting in an extended period of below normal temperatures. The \ncoolest days will be today and Tuesday when highs will range from \nthe upper 50s to the lower 60s in most areas...and nightly lows will \ngenerally range through the 40s. The exception to the latter will be \nacross the North Country where skies should clear enough tonight/ \nremain clear enough Tuesday night to allow lows in the upper 30s... \nwhich may allow for some isolated frost in a few spots. Gradual day-\nto-day warming will then follow for the rest of the week as our \nairmass slowly modifies. \n\nWith respect to precipitation chances...the approaching Ohio Valley \nshortwaves/developing closed upper low should encourage weak surface \ntroughing to our south to temporarily edge back northward again \nbetween Monday night and Wednesday...when moisture/lift from these \nfeatures may allowing for a few spotty light showers at times across \nthe Southern Tier. Otherwise the strong surface high and largely \nconfluent flow aloft should keep our region dry through the work \nweek...before the aforementioned coastal system potentially throws a \nfew showers back across our eastern zones next weekend...with this \npotential dependent upon just how far west this feature ultimately \ngets.",
